le paths. The problem is the file I/O and the OpenSSL-specific key loading. Let me think about how to handle this.\n\nLooking at the C code, the main steps are reading the config file as bytes, extracting the kem_id from a specific byte range, and loading the private key from a PEM file using OpenSSL. In Rust, I can handle the file I/O straightforwardly with `std::fs::read`, but I'll need to figure out the key loading piece — either using the `pem` crate or an OpenSSL binding to parse the private key data. for the config bytes and handle the key loading.\n\nActually, looking at the C code, it extracts bytes 7 and 8 from the config to get the kem_id. Let me trace through the ECH config format more carefully. The structure starts with a 2-byte outer length wrapper, then the ECHConfig itself which has version (0xFE0D for draft-13), an inner length field, config_id, and then the kem_id at bytes 7-9. So the C code's byte indexing matches up perfectly with this layout.\n\nFor the Rust side, I need to read the binary ECHConfigList file, parse out the kem_id from the right offset, load the corresponding private key from a PEM file, and construct the EchOpenerState. The C implementation uses a function pointer for key loading that I'll need to replicate in Rust. I need to figure out how to load the ECH configuration and private key files in Rust without adding too many dependencies. The approach would be reading the binary config file and prepending a 2-byte length header, then reading the PEM key file and extracting the raw key bytes. Let me check if the existing OpenSSL bindings in the codebase already have key-loading utilities I can leverage.\n\nFor the `picoquic_ech_create_config_from_pk` function, I'm looking at a more complex operation that derives the KEM from the curve, determines cipher suites, computes the config ID, and encodes everything using picotls APIs — this will definitely require the picotls binding layer. I should examine the sys module structure and the